      "excerpt": "Same core CSL DD base, very different torque headroom. Here is who should actually pay for 8 Nm.",
      "score": "10 MIN",
      "art": "sim",
      "quick": "If you already know you enjoy sim racing and have a solid mount, 8 Nm is the better long-term CSL DD setup. The 5 Nm version is still a sensible way to enter the ecosystem because the base can be upgraded later with Fanatec's Boost Kit 180.",
      "body": "\n        <h2>Same base, different power supply</h2>\n        <p>Fanatec's CSL DD QR2 base is rated at 5 Nm in standard form. The 8 Nm Ready2Race package includes the Boost Kit 180 power supply, unlocking the higher torque mode.</p>\n        <h2>What you feel at 8 Nm</h2>\n        <p>The main gain is headroom. Strong corners and sudden force-feedback peaks are less likely to flatten against the base's torque ceiling, so detail can remain more distinct at higher overall strength.</p>\n        <h2>When 5 Nm makes sense</h2>\n        <p>Choose 5 Nm when you want the lowest entry price, you are desk mounting, or you want to confirm that sim racing sticks before spending more.</p>\n        <h2>When to buy 8 Nm immediately</h2>\n        <p>If you have a rigid cockpit and expect to keep the base for years, buying the 8 Nm package avoids an early upgrade and better matches load-cell pedals and a more serious rig.</p><h2>Why the 8 Nm version feels like more than a 60% number</h2><p>The benefit is not that you should run every car at maximum force. The extra ceiling lets you keep a comfortable baseline while leaving more room for brief peaks. That can preserve detail in loaded corners instead of compressing everything near the limit.</p><h2>When the upgrade should wait</h2><p>If the wheel is on a flexible desk or the brake pedal is still sliding, the Boost Kit is not automatically the best next purchase. A stable platform or better braking can improve consistency more than extra steering force.</p><h2>Buying strategy</h2><p>New buyers who already have a rigid cockpit should compare the cost of the 8 Nm bundle with buying 5 Nm and upgrading later. Existing 5 Nm owners can treat the Boost Kit as a simple way to extend the life of the same base rather than replacing the whole system.</p><h2>Compatibility lives across the ecosystem</h2><p>Fanatec console support can depend on the wheelbase and steering wheel combination. PC is straightforward, while Xbox functionality is commonly tied to an Xbox-licensed wheel. PlayStation compatibility requires appropriately licensed base hardware, so do not assume the CSL DD can become a PlayStation base with a different rim.</p><h2>Power supply is the key difference</h2><p>The 8 Nm mode is unlocked through the appropriate Boost Kit power supply. That makes the upgrade unusually simple compared with replacing a wheelbase, but it also means buyers should compare official bundle pricing rather than assuming a later upgrade will always cost the same.</p><h2>Value verdict</h2><p>If the 8 Nm package is only modestly more expensive and the rig is ready, buy once. If budget is tight, 5 Nm keeps the door open and lets you spend first on the mechanical parts of the setup that determine consistency.</p>",
