      "body": "<h2>Why 2TB is such a useful gaming capacity</h2><p>Modern game installs can easily run into triple-digit gigabytes. A 1TB system drive can feel cramped once Windows, launchers, captures and a handful of large games share the same space. Two terabytes gives most players enough breathing room without paying the steep price of top-end 4TB models.</p><h2>Best overall: Samsung 990 Pro 2TB</h2><p>Samsung's current data sheet rates the 2TB 990 Pro at up to 7,450 MB/s reads and 6,900 MB/s writes, with TLC NAND, DRAM cache and a five-year limited warranty. It remains a mature, fast Gen4 choice and is still Tom's Hardware's overall/gaming SSD pick in 2026.</p><p><strong>Why buy it:</strong> balanced premium performance, excellent software support and broad PC/PS5 suitability. <strong>Why skip it:</strong> another quality Gen4 drive can be the better purchase if Samsung pricing jumps.</p><h2>Best alternative: WD Black SN850X 2TB</h2><p>The SN850X is close enough in real-world class that price should often decide it. SanDisk lists up to 7,300 MB/s reads and 6,600 MB/s writes for the 2TB model, with 1,200 TBW endurance and a five-year warranty. Heatsink variants also make it an easy PS5 shortlist.</p><h2>Best third option: Crucial T500 2TB</h2><p>Crucial rates the 2TB T500 at up to 7,400 MB/s reads and 7,000 MB/s writes with TLC NAND and 1,200 TBW endurance. It is exactly the sort of drive to check when Amazon pricing makes the headline Samsung and WD models look expensive.</p><h2>Do gamers need PCIe 5.0?</h2><p>Usually not. Gen5 drives can be spectacular in sequential benchmarks and heavy workstation tasks, but game performance is not doubled just because peak transfer speed is. A mature Gen4 SSD still gives fast loading, strong responsiveness and lower platform demands for most gaming builds.</p><h2>Heatsink or no heatsink?</h2><p>On PC, many motherboards already include an M.2 heatsink. Buying a second bulky heatsink can create fit problems, so check the board first. For PS5, make sure the installed drive has appropriate cooling and fits the console's M.2 bay requirements.</p><h2>How to shop this guide</h2><p>We deliberately do not publish a fixed Amazon price because SSD pricing moves constantly. Open all three current listings, compare the same 2TB capacity and heatsink configuration, and buy the strongest value that day.</p>",
